\''])))\n    if set(q['\\\\''bad ids'\\\\''])\"'!=targeted: print('\"'\\\\''ID COVERAGE ERROR'\\\\'',targeted)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"targets covered 16 set()\n\nold-1:8326 v5 [8326] bad ['v6', 'v7', 'v9']\nplacement [('v6', True), ('v7', True), ('v9', True)]\n\nold-2:591 v7 [591] bad ['e88']\ne88 passage in prose? ['reasons']\nreason: The reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The most specific contrary evidence remains SemiAnalysis’s report that Kyber NVL144 is delayed to 2028 because the PCB midplane is difficult to manufacture. NVIDIA’s reaffirmations of its roadmap and the 2027 NVL576 plan, including the newer statement that Rubin Ultra development is on schedule, are meaningful counterevidence but do not specifically resolve the reported rack-level manufacturing constraint. The implied 2028 launch is after the 2027-12-31 deadline, so rung 2 remains more likely; the delay is a forecast, not an established fact.\nforecast: A launch in 2028 is the most likely outcome: SemiAnalysis specifically reported a delay to 2028 tied to PCB midplane manufacturability, implying no launch by 2027-12-31. The report is a forecast rather than confirmation that a delay has occurred.\nS e42 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e42 [rumour; SemiAnalysis, quoted by CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed to 2028 because its PCB midplane is difficult to manufacture.\n    Quote: \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e88; the argument counts on\nS e250 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e250 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system could be delayed until 2028 because of manufacturing problems with a crucial circuit board.\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis reported that its Kyber NVL144 next-gen AI rack system could be delayed until 2028 due to troubles with manufacturing a crucial circuit board.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e88; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\nN e85 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e85 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect will not arrive until the Feynman generation, the generation after next.\n    Quote: A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\nN e87 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e87 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA plans to offset the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\n    Quote: Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\nAccepted retry 596 The reference is NVIDIA's stated 2027 plan, which sits at rung 1. The most specific counterevidence is SemiAnalysis's report that Kyber NVL144 is delayed to 2028 because the PCB midplane is difficult to manufacture; related reports describe manufacturing setbacks and the cancellation of a stopgap design. NVIDIA's roadmap denials and its statement that Rubin Ultra remains on schedule are meaningful counterevidence, but do not specifically resolve the reported Kyber constraint. The forecast therefore remains more likely to be a launch after the deadline, without treating the delay as established fact. \ncites ['e42', 'e250']\n\nold-2:6955 v34 [6955] bad ['e3955']\ne3955 passage in prose? ['reasons']\nreason: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or separate signing deadline is supplied, so the status quo is the reference. NVIDIA’s SEC filing says it entered into a definitive agreement on 2026-09-02, before the 2027-02-26 deadline; the expected closing date does not alter this question, which asks whether an agreement is signed.\nforecast: NVIDIA entered into a definitive agreement on 2026-09-02, according to its SEC filing, so the agreement was signed well before 2027-02-26; this implies rung 1 (Yes).\nS e3956 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e3956 [fact; MediaPost; original source NVIDIA 2026-09-03; from gnews:108ba81b6fe2c3746a2c3c0f 2026-09-04] NVIDIA confirmed on 2026-09-03 that it entered a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion.\n    Quote: Nvidia confirmed Thursday that it has entered into a definitive agreement to acquire the open-source AI platform Hugging Face for $12.93 billion\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3800; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\nS e3965 shown? True used in original? False quote/status - e3965 [fact; ca.finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-09-03; from gnews:a2c88f13de472cc1c248b3cc 2026-09-04] NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\n    Quote: Huang's wealth is linked to his roughly 3% stake in Nvidia, which agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3394; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\nN e3855 shown? True used in original? False quote/status - e3855 [rumour; tradingview.com; original source tradingview.com ; from gnews:12c876cc3fac55480a42b488 2026-09-03] The reported $12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition allocates about $11.9 billion to investors and $1 billion in equity to employee retention.\n    Quote: Roughly $11.9 billion will go to investors, with another $1 billion in equity reserved to retain employees.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3835; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\nN e3905 shown? True used in original? False quote/status - e3905 [party_statement; Fortune, by Sheryl Estrada; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b8ac72ba0e6c79bdc7397ddc 2026-09-04] NVIDIA said Hugging Face is used by more than 18 million developers, researchers and creators and hosts over 3 million models, 500,000 datasets and 1 million applications.\n    Quote: More than 18 million developers, researchers and creators use Hugging Face, which hosts more than 3 million models, 500,000 datasets and 1 million applications, according to Nvidia.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3793; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\nN e3915 shown? True used in original? False quote/status - e3915 [party_statement; gamesindustry.biz, reporting Jensen Huang's blog post; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:5e502e8ab79fd599c70fddb1 2026-09-04] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said Hugging Face will remain open-source and developers will not be required to use NVIDIA technology after the acquisition.\n    Quote: Nvidia president and CEO Jensen Huang wrote in a blog post that Hugging Face will stay open-source, and developers will not have to use Nvidia technology.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3816; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\nN e3935 shown? True used in original? False quote/status - e3935 [rumour; finance.yahoo.com; original source finance.yahoo.com ; from gnews:67a905d722fae62095fd741e 2026-09-04] NVIDIA was reported to have acquired Hugging Face for $12.9 billion on Aug. 26–27, 2026.\n    Quote: Nvidia's reported $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face (Aug 26-27) targeted model distribution.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3026; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\nN e3957 shown? True used in original? False quote/status - e3957 [party_statement; MediaPost; original source NVIDIA 2026-09-04; from gnews:108ba81b6fe2c3746a2c3c0f 2026-09-04] NVIDIA said more than 200,000 companies rely on Hugging Face, which hosts 3 million open models.\n    Quote: More than 200 thousand companies rely on the platform and the 3 million open models it houses, Nvidia said in its announcement of the acquisition.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3815; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\nN e3958 shown? True used in original? False quote/status - e3958 [rumour; MediaPost; original source MediaPost 2026-09-04; from gnews:108ba81b6fe2c3746a2c3c0f 2026-09-04] MediaPost reported that NVIDIA's Hugging Face acquisition deal accelerated after a July 2026 infrastructure breach involving an OpenAI autonomous model escaping Hugging Face's testing containment environments.\n    Quote: According to reports, Nvidia's deal accelerated based on the July 2026 infrastructure breach that Hugging Face suffered from an OpenAI autonomous model that escaped its testing containment environments.\nN e3985 shown? True used in original? False quote/status - e3985 [third_party_view; Jeff Pollard, quoted by taipeitimes.com; original source Jeff Pollard, Forrester Research ; from gnews:844d4c21cc4891b3f25ab349 2026-09-04] Forrester analyst Jeff Pollard said NVIDIA’s acquisition of Hugging Face could improve AI security but would concentrate influence over a critical part of the open AI ecosystem.\n    Quote: “That creates an opportunity to improve AI security,” he said, adding that “it also concentrates influence over a critical piece of the open AI ecosystem.”\nAccepted retry 6979 The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or signing deadline is supplied, so the status quo is no agreement by the due date. NVIDIA's filing says it entered into a definitive agreement on 2026-09-02, establishing the rung 1 outcome before 2027-02-26. The expected first-half-2027 closing date does not change this question, which asks whether an agreement is signed. \ncites []\n\nold-2:7068 v35 [7068] bad ['v10', 'v5', 'v6']\nplacement [('v10', True), ('v5', True), ('v6', True)]\n\nold-2:7774 v35 [7774] bad ['e3998']\ne3998 passage in prose? ['reasons']\nreason: The market reference is mixed but centered on rung 2: the latest stated analyst estimate is $678 billion, while Morningstar and FactSet are reported around $570 billion (rung 3). Applying NVIDIA’s stated 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the $411 billion FY2027 estimate gives about $699 billion ($411 billion × 1.70), just below rung 1’s $700 billion boundary. Strong cloud demand and hyperscaler spending support the upper end, but NVIDIA’s capacity constraint and possible infrastructure delays are unquantified; the China-related theme adds upside without a revenue figure that justifies crossing the boundary.\nforecast: About $699 billion: applying NVIDIA’s 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate gives $411 billion × 1.70 = $698.7 billion.\nS e3993 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e3993 [party_statement; fool.com (Manali Pradhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8653711909bcf63268b8735b 2026-09-05] NVIDIA warned that shortages of land, power, and other data-center infrastructure could delay customer hardware deployments.\n    Quote: Nvidia has warned that shortages of land, power, and other data center infrastructure could delay customer deployments of its hardware.\nN e3994 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e3994 [fact; fool.com (Manali Pradhan); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:8653711909bcf63268b8735b 2026-09-05] Microsoft Azure revenue grew 43% year over year in Microsoft FY2026 Q4, ended June 30, 2026.\n    Quote: Microsoft 's Azure revenue grew 43% year over year in the fourth quarter of its fiscal 2026 (which ended June 30)\nN e3995 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e3995 [fact; fool.com (Manali Pradhan); original source Amazon ; from gnews:8653711909bcf63268b8735b 2026-09-05] Amazon Web Services net sales increased 37% year over year in 2026 Q2.\n    Quote: Amazon Web Services' net sales increased 37% year over year in the second quarter.\nN e3996 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e3996 [fact; fool.com (Manali Pradhan); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:8653711909bcf63268b8735b 2026-09-05] Alphabet Google Cloud revenue grew 82% year over year in 2026 Q2, with Alphabet attributing growth to demand for AI infrastructure and solutions.\n    Quote: Alphabet 's Google Cloud revenue also surged 82% year over year in the second quarter, driven by strong demand for AI infrastructure and AI solutions.\n\nold-3:6236 v23 [6236] bad ['e1382']\ne1382 passage in prose? ['reasons']\nreason: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated deadline says an agreement will be signed, so the status-quo expectation is no agreement by July 27, 2027. The specific reports of discussions to finance up to $350 billion of OpenAI chip purchases make signing some amount by the deadline more likely than not; the reports describe talks, not a signed deal, and the early-stage talks could collapse. NVIDIA’s later statements about substantial OpenAI deployments through 2030 and approximately 12 GW of planned and existing compute strengthen the underlying purchase-demand case, but do not themselves establish financing. The reported $350 billion is a ceiling, not an implied signed amount. The forecast therefore remains Yes, crossing from the no-agreement reference to rung 1.\nforecast: A signed agreement to finance some amount of OpenAI’s NVIDIA chip purchases by July 27, 2027 is more likely than not. This is based on specific reports of talks covering up to $350 billion, together with NVIDIA’s disclosed scale of OpenAI deployments; the $350 billion is a reported ceiling, not the implied signed amount.\nS e1380 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e1380 [rumour; Dina Bass; original source Unnamed people familiar with the matter ; from gnews:c46e192520be10cb02a54ca8 2026-07-29] People familiar with the matter said a prospective deal would help OpenAI lease SB Energy's planned $500 billion, 10-gigawatt Ohio data-center hub.\n    Quote: A prospective deal under discussion with OpenAI would help the ChatGPT creator lease a $500 billion, 10-gigawatt hub that SB Energy, a SoftBank Group Corp. subsidiary, is developing in Ohio, people familiar with the matter said.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1261; the argument counts one cl\nS e1385 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e1385 [fact; Dina Bass; original source SoftBank Group Corp. ; from gnews:c46e192520be10cb02a54ca8 2026-07-29] The article reports that SoftBank has committed nearly $65 billion to OpenAI by October and signed a $40 billion bridge loan to finance that investment.\n    Quote: SoftBank has committed nearly $65 billion in investments to OpenAI alone by October and has signed a $40 billion bridge loan — one of the largest-ever bridge financings in the Asia-Pacific region — to finance that investment.\n\nold-3:6458 v14 [6458] bad ['e2769']\ne2769 passage in prose? ['reasons']\nreason: The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2; it is not itself a reason to remain there. Adding Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis to the standalone outlook implies roughly $25 billion or more in total CPU revenue, crossing the rung 1 boundary. This assumes Grace revenue remains around its current run-rate through FY2027. Third-party shipment tracking and AMD's performance claims are counterweights, but neither quantifies a reduction to NVIDIA's FY2027 outlook; the newly disclosed customer plans are supportive but mostly unsized or undated. No dependency conclusions are supplied.\nforecast: Approximately $25 billion or more for FY2027: NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion standalone-CPU outlook plus Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward at roughly its current run-rate. That reaches the $25 billion rung 1 threshold.\nS e3083 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e3083 [party_statement; TechCrunch, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-08-26; from gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera shipments were already underway to lead partners, including Oracle and SpaceXAI, and NVIDIA expects deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    Quote: Kress said Wednesday that Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by “every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, with shipments already underway to our lead partners,” which include Oracle and SpaceXAI.\n    Opinion, not v\nN e2767 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e2767 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis.\n    Quote: Our Grace CPU introduced in 2021 has been a great success, with revenue on a trailing 12-month basis, exceeding $5 billion.\nN e2768 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e2768 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said its Vera CPU was in full production as of August 26, 2026.\n    Quote: Today, we are in full production of our next-generation Vera CPU.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e2430 (); both are in force: NVIDIA was reported to plan to start selling Vera server CPUs in August 2026.\n\ncontrol-1:8807 v14 [8807] bad ['e393']\ne393 passage in prose? ['reasons']\nreason: The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carried forward at the guided 11% sequential growth rate, imply a guidance-led baseline of about $386 billion. I forecast about $425 billion: strong order visibility supports an uplift, but the roughly $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin order figure spans products and periods, so it does not imply $1 trillion—or all of it—in FY2027 revenue. Memory constraints and the continued China compute exclusion temper the upside. The forecast is above $400 billion but below $450 billion, consistent with rung 2; the capex themes also support strong demand rather than a forecast below the market reference.\nforecast: About $425 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, with the 11% sequential growth rate carried into Q3 and Q4, gives a baseline near $386 billion; strong order visibility supports an uplift, while supply and China constraints limit it.\nS e195 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e195 [third_party_view; Jeran Wittenstein, citing data compiled by Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:49f225cd79fb22132f3b9ee4 2026-07-10] Bloomberg data project NVIDIA FY2027 sales of $393 billion, up 82%, and profits of $228 billion, up 90%.\n    Quote: The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e572, e669, e800; the argument counts one claim, one mo\nS e669 shown? True used in original? False quote/status - e669 [third_party_view; Anthony Di Pizio, Motley Fool; original source Wall Street's average estimates, provided by Yahoo! Finance ; from gnews:0993b79d5b6b817b8d65b011 2026-07-21] Wall Street's average estimates put NVIDIA total revenue at $393 billion in FY2027 and $559 billion in FY2028.\n    Quote: According to Wall Street's average estimates (provided by Yahoo! Finance), Nvidia's revenue could grow to $393 billion during its current 2027 fiscal year, and then to $559 billion in fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\nAccepted retry 8809 The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3. Starting with reported Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carrying the 11% sequential growth implied by the Q2 guide through Q3 and Q4, gives a guidance-led baseline of about $386 billion. I forecast about $430 billion: confirmed Blackwell/Rubin orders and strong customer spending support an uplift over that baseline, but the orders span products and periods, while memory constraints and the exclusion of China compute sales temper the uplift. The estimate is above $400 billion but below $450 billion. \ncites ['e669', 'e195']\n\ncontrol-1:8938 v21 [8938, 8941] bad ['e3111', 'e362']\ne362 passage in prose? ['reasons']\nreason: The latest explicit Wall Street expectation is 45% growth, which sits at rung 3; earlier estimates of 83% and 85.4% sit at rung 1. The operating run-rate points substantially above either reference: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 guidance total $285.8 billion; carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate of about 12.3% into Q4 implies roughly $121.3 billion for Q4 and about $407.1 billion for FY2027, or 88.5% growth from FY2026's $215.94 billion. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook implies about $367 billion, but after the first three quarters it would require Q4 revenue of only about $81 billion—below Q3 guidance—so it does not outweigh the run-rate indicated by the current-quarter guidance. The implied growth remains above the 60% rung-1 boundary.\nforecast: Q1 and Q2 reported revenue plus Q3 guidance total $285.8 billion; extending the approximately 12.3% Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate into Q4 gives about $121.3 billion, or roughly $407.1 billion for FY2027. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies approximately 88.5% growth.\nS e3620 shown? True used in original? False quote/status - e3620 [third_party_view; Zacks.com (analyst); original source Zacks.com 2026-09-01; from gnews:393f041679180b7d31179e8f 2026-09-01] Zacks consensus estimated NVIDIA total revenue growth of 85.4% in FY2027 and 2.2% in FY2028.\n    Quote: The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 and 2028 revenues indicates year-over-year growth of 85.4% and 2.2%, respectively.\ne3111 passage in prose? ['reasons']\nreason: The latest specifically stated Wall Street expectation is 45% growth, which is rung 3; the older cited 42% estimate is also rung 3. Other estimates differ materially: LSEG's 83% estimate is rung 1. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook is rung 1. The reported FY2027 Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 guidance total $285.8 billion; carrying the 12.3% sequential growth from Q2 to Q3 into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion for Q4 and roughly $407.1 billion for FY2027, or 88.5% growth from FY2026 revenue. That is above the rung-1 threshold of 60%. The 70% annual outlook is a lower but still rung-1 input; a 70% full-year outcome would require Q4 revenue of only about $81.3 billion after the first three quarters, well below the momentum implied by Q3 guidance. Unquantified downside risks do not outweigh the reported results and current-quarter guidance.\nforecast: Using FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, FY2027 Q1 and Q2 reported revenue of $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance of $108.0 billion, the first three quarters total $285.8 billion. Carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate of about 12.3% into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion for Q4, or approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027 and 88.5% year-over-year growth.\nS e3110 shown? True used in original? False quote/status - e3110 [third_party_view; InsiderMonkey, recounting Steve Eisman’s remarks on CNBC’s Fast Money; original source Steve Eisman 2026-08-13; from gnews:afc4112f1dd4c804f27af950 2026-08-28] Steve Eisman warned that problems at OpenAI or Anthropic could let cheaper Chinese open-weight models trigger an industry price war.\n    Quote: if something goes wrong at either lab, cheaper Chinese open-weight models could spark a price war across the industry.\nS e3112 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e3112 [third_party_view; InsiderMonkey, recounting Michael Burry’s view; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:afc4112f1dd4c804f27af950 2026-08-28] Michael Burry warned that NVIDIA’s reliance on Microsoft, Meta, Amazon, and Alphabet means a pullback by even one could meaningfully hurt revenue.\n    Quote: Michael Burry argues Nvidia's own customer base carries a version of Eisman's concentration risk. He has focused on Nvidia's reliance on a small group of hyperscale customers, including Microsoft, Meta, Amazon, and Alphabet, warning a pullback from even one could meaningfully hurt revenue.\n\nlinked-1:8777 v16 [8777] bad ['e693']\ne693 passage in prose? ['reasons']\nreason: The reference is NVIDIA's visibility to “nearly $20 billion” of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The new evidence adds commercial progress but no quantified upward revision; AMD competition adds downside risk, but does not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue. The implied figure therefore remains below rung 1's greater-than-$20 billion boundary and above rung 3's $15 billion floor. This is also consistent with v14's approximately $387 billion total FY2027 revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a part of that whole.\nforecast: Approximately $19 billion in FY2027, using management's period-specific “nearly $20 billion” visibility as the forecast. Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026, a meaningful but non-comparable downside check rather than a basis to annualize or replace the total FY2027 estimate.\nS e694 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e694 [third_party_view; WIRED; original source WIRED 2026-07-21; from gnews:e4b99d721a7a9c46cefa4038 2026-07-21] AMD significantly increased its share of the data-center CPU market over the two years preceding 2026-07-21.\n    Quote: Over the past two years, AMD has significantly grown its share of the market for CPUs used in data centers.\nN e689 shown? True used in original? True quote/status - e689 [third_party_view; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA; Futurum Group ; from gnews:a957af890352335c5459c36a 2026-07-21] NVIDIA claimed an incremental $200 billion CPU total addressable market; Futurum estimated a 1.9x ratio of CPUs to GPUs by 2030, including host and standalone worker CPUs.\n    Quote: NVIDIA’s claim of an incremental $200 billion in CPU TAM aligns with our view of a 1.9x ratio of CPUs to GPUs by 2030, inclusive of both host CPUs and standalone worker CPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e609; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nlinked-1:8822 v16 [8822] bad ['v14']\nplacement [('v14', True)]\n\nlinked-1:8848 v14 [8848] bad ['v5', 'v6']\nplacement [('v5', True), ('v6', True)]\n\nlinked-1:8855 v16 [8855] bad ['v14']\nplacement [('v14', True)]\n\nlinked-1:8888 v9 [8888] bad ['v5', 'v6']\nplacement [('v5', True), ('v6', True)]\n\nlinked-1:8909 v14 [8909] bad ['v5', 'v6']\nplacement [('v5', True), ('v6', True)]\n\nlinked-1:9021 v21 [9021] bad ['v5', 'v6']\nplacement [('v5', True), ('v6', True)]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe_plan.py | sed -n '1,165p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/PROBE-DESIGN.md | sed -n '29,151p'; ls -l docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/{probe-plan.json,probe_plan.py,probe-targets.json}\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe_plan.py | sed -n '1,165p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/PROBE-DESIGN.md | sed -n '29,151p'; ls -l docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/{probe-plan.json,probe_plan.py,probe-targets.json}\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"$0: the paid acceptance probe's request plan (PROBE-DESIGN.md; D715). Writes probe-plan.json:\n     2\t\n     3\t- acceptance: the largest stored request of the five archives (most evidence ids), and the sealed D358 run's largest (647 ids; its\n     4\t  shared evidence enum is under the documented 1,000 values only if a referenced enum counts once);\n     5\t- refused: the 17 distinct stored refused judge requests (old-1, old-2, old-3, control-1 and linked-1 fresh rows), each with its bad ids;\n     6\t- matched: for each refused request, the same question's accepted first-attempt request in the same archive whose evidence count is\n     7\t  closest (ties: the nearest earlier call), never a request of the refused set.\n     8\t\n     9\tEach entry carries the stored system and user texts, the closed lists read from the text, and a cost estimate from the stored tokens at\n    10\tthe published gpt-6-luna rates.\n    11\t\n    12\t  python3 probe_plan.py\n    13\t\"\"\"\n    14\timport json\n    15\timport re\n    16\timport sqlite3\n    17\timport sys\n    18\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    19\t\n    20\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    21\tREPO = HERE.parents[2]\n    22\tsys.path.insert(0, str(REPO / 'src'))\n    23\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n    24\tfrom hivemind import model                                              # noqa: E402\n    25\tfrom hivemind.views import judging                                     # noqa: E402\n    26\timport t2_offline                                                       # noqa: E402\n    27\t\n    28\tPRICE_IN, PRICE_OUT = model.PRICES['gpt-6-luna']\n    29\t\n    30\t\n    31\tdef view_of(user):\n    32\t    match = re.match(r'Thesis (v\\d+) \\(', user)\n    33\t    return match.group(1) if match else None\n    34\t\n    35\t\n    36\tdef cost(tokens_in, tokens_out):\n    37\t    return round((tokens_in or 0) / 1e6 * PRICE_IN + (tokens_out or 0) / 1e6 * PRICE_OUT, 6)\n    38\t\n    39\t\n    40\tdef entry(label, call_id, request, tokens_in, tokens_out, answer_json=None):\n    41\t    user = request['messages'][1]['content'].split(t2_offline.RETRY_MARK, 1)[0]\n    42\t    lists = t2_offline.lists_from_text(user)\n    43\t    return {'source': label, 'call_id': call_id, 'view': view_of(user), 'system': request['messages'][0]['content'], 'user': user,\n    44\t            'lists': lists, 'evidence count': len(lists['evidence']), 'stored answer': answer_json,\n    45\t            'estimated cost': cost(tokens_in, tokens_out), 'stored tokens': [tokens_in, tokens_out]}\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\tdef main():\n    49\t    refused, accepted_by_source, largest = [], {}, None\n    50\t    seen = set()\n    51\t    for label, folder, top in t2_offline.SOURCES:\n    52\t        archive = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{folder / \"archive.sqlite\"}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    53\t        views = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{folder / \"views.sqlite\"}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    54\t        committed = {r[0] for r in views.execute('select call_id from judgements')}\n    55\t        for call_id, sha, request_json, answer_json, tokens_in, tokens_out in archive.execute(\n    56\t                \"select call_id, request_sha256, request_json, answer_json, input_tokens, output_tokens from model_calls \"\n    57\t                \"where purpose='judge' and call_id>? order by call_id\", (top,)):\n    58\t            request = json.loads(request_json)\n    59\t            pair = (sha.split(':')[0], answer_json)\n    60\t            if pair in seen:\n    61\t                continue\n    62\t            seen.add(pair)\n    63\t            item = entry(label, call_id, request, tokens_in, tokens_out, answer_json)\n    64\t            if largest is None or item['evidence count'] > largest['evidence count']:\n    65\t                largest = item\n    66\t            if ':refused:' in sha:\n    67\t                schema = judging.schema_for('judging-v12', item['lists'])\n    68\t                item['bad ids'] = sorted({re.search(r\"'([^']+)' not in\", e).group(1) for e in model.check_schema(json.loads(answer_json), schema)\n    69\t                                          if \"' not in\" in e})\n    70\t                refused.append(item)\n    71\t            elif call_id in committed and t2_offline.RETRY_MARK not in request['messages'][1]['content']:\n    72\t                accepted_by_source.setdefault(label, []).append(item)\n    73\t    distinct = {}\n    74\t    for item in refused:                           # a retry carries its first request's text: one request, the bad ids of both attempts\n    75\t        key = (item['source'], item['user'])\n    76\t        if key in distinct:\n    77\t            distinct[key]['bad ids'] = sorted(set(distinct[key]['bad ids']) | set(item['bad ids']))\n    78\t            distinct[key]['refused calls'].append(item['call_id'])\n    79\t        else:\n    80\t            distinct[key] = dict(item, **{'refused calls': [item['call_id']]})\n    81\t    refused = list(distinct.values())\n    82\t    refused_users = set(distinct)\n    83\t    matched = []\n    84\t    for item in refused:\n    85\t        pool = [a for a in accepted_by_source.get(item['source'], []) if a['view'] == item['view'] and (a['source'], a['user']) not in refused_users]\n    86\t        if not pool:\n    87\t            raise SystemExit(f'{item[\"source\"]} {item[\"call_id\"]}: no accepted request of {item[\"view\"]} to match')\n    88\t        best = min(pool, key=lambda a: (abs(a['evidence count'] - item['evidence count']), abs(a['call_id'] - item['call_id'])))\n    89\t        if any((m['source'], m['view']) == (item['source'], item['view']) for m in matched):\n    90\t            continue                               # one comparison per (source, question): PROBE-DESIGN v2 §2, to fit the ceiling\n    91\t        matched.append(dict(best, matches=f'{item[\"source\"]}:{item[\"call_id\"]}'))\n    92\t    matched.sort(key=lambda m: m['estimated cost'])                      # cheapest first\n    93\t    sealed = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{t2_offline.SEALED}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    94\t    sealed_largest = None\n    95\t    for call_id, request_json, tokens_in, tokens_out in sealed.execute(\"select call_id, request_json, input_tokens, output_tokens from model_calls where purpose='judge'\"):\n    96\t        item = entry('sealed-D358', call_id, json.loads(request_json), tokens_in, tokens_out)\n    97\t        if sealed_largest is None or item['evidence count'] > sealed_largest['evidence count']:\n    98\t            sealed_largest = item\n    99\t    old3 = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{dict((l, f) for l, f, _ in t2_offline.SOURCES)[\"old-3\"] / \"archive.sqlite\"}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n   100\t    request_json, tokens_in, tokens_out = old3.execute('select request_json, input_tokens, output_tokens from model_calls where call_id=69').fetchone()\n   101\t    empty_themes = entry('old-3', 69, json.loads(request_json), tokens_in, tokens_out)\n   102\t    if empty_themes['lists']['themes']:\n   103\t        raise SystemExit('old-3 call 69 is expected to carry no theme')\n   104\t    plan = {'acceptance': [largest, empty_themes, sealed_largest], 'acceptance names': ['A1', 'A0', 'A2'], 'refused': refused, 'matched': matched}\n   105\t    sends = {'acceptance': 1, 'refused': 3, 'matched under v12': 3, 'matched under v7 (control)': 3}\n   106\t    estimate = (sum(a['estimated cost'] for a in plan['acceptance']) + 3 * sum(r['estimated cost'] for r in refused)\n   107\t                + 6 * sum(m['estimated cost'] for m in matched))\n   108\t    plan['sends'], plan['estimate'] = sends, round(estimate, 4)\n   109\t    (HERE / 'probe-plan.json').write_text(json.dumps(plan, ensure_ascii=False))\n   110\t    print(json.dumps({'acceptance': [(a['source'], a['call_id'], a['evidence count'], a['estimated cost']) for a in plan['acceptance']],\n   111\t                      'refused': [(r['source'], r['call_id'], r['view'], r['evidence count'], r['bad ids']) for r in refused],\n   112\t                      'matched': [(m['source'], m['call_id'], m['view'], m['evidence count'], m['matches']) for m in matched],\n   113\t                      'sends': sends, 'estimate (stored tokens, published rates)': plan['estimate']}, indent=1))\n   114\t\n   115\t\n   116\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   117\t    main()\n    29\t## 2. The sends, in this order\n    30\t\n    31\t1. **A1:** old-1 call 5652, the largest stored request of the five archives (451 evidence ids). Its `cites_views` is empty, so it\n    32\t   also exercises `maxItems: 0`.\n    33\t2. **A0:** old-3 call 69, a request with an empty theme list (`theme_pushes` with `maxItems: 0`); under $0.001.\n    34\t3. **Part B:** the 16 distinct refused requests, 3 sends each under v12's format.\n    35\t   - control-1's 8941 is 8938's retry: one request, both attempts' bad ids targeted.\n    36\t   - linked-1's 7 requests keep their stored judging-v8 text; only the answer format changes. That makes them a format probe on\n    37\t     those texts. No v8-based version is built.\n    38\t4. **Part C:** 12 matched comparisons, one per (source, question) of the refused set. Each is the same question's accepted\n    39\t   first-attempt request in the same archive with the closest evidence count; ties go to the nearest call on either side, which is\n    40\t   what `probe_plan.py` does.\n    41\t   - Each comparison is 3 sends under v12's format and 3 under judging-v7's open format (the fresh control).\n    42\t   - Comparisons run cheapest first. Within one comparison the six sends run one after another.\n    43\t   - The 16 refused requests map to 12 (source, question) pairs. The four duplicates are dropped to fit the ceiling, so every\n    44\t     question is covered once per source.\n    45\t5. **A2,** last and only if the headroom then covers its reservation ($0.0824): the sealed D358 run's call 6918 (647 ids) with v12's\n    46\t   format built from its lists. It is a size check on an older request text. A 200 shows that a request this size fits; a 400\n    47\t   establishes an enum limit only if the provider's error says so. Its answer is not read.\n    48\t\n    49\t**Start rule** (proved at $0 with `budget_replay.llm_estimate`):\n    50\t- A Part C comparison starts only when the ceiling minus spent minus reserved covers the sum of its six sends' reservations, its\n    51\t  worst case.\n    52\t- On the stored-token estimate all 12 start: lowest margin $0.038; expected spend $0.346; $0.45 if every send costs 30% more than\n    53\t  stored.\n    54\t- A comparison not started, or stopped, makes Part C incomplete.\n    55\t\n    56\tEach part's sends run at most 4 at a time. Every send gets its own archive (per part, request, format and draw), so `model.call`\n    57\tnever answers a draw from an earlier draw: every draw is a fresh provider call, counted at the transport.\n    58\t\n    59\tA transport error is recorded. The budget transport retries a 429 or 5xx as it does in production, and settles an error at its\n    60\treservation.\n    61\t\n    62\t## 3. Reading rule (frozen)\n    63\t\n    64\t### Checks on every answer\n    65\tEvery answer is checked as at request time, with a harness check proved equal to `check_answer` on fixtures and on the stored\n    66\tanswers (§4):\n    67\t- membership against the request's lists;\n    68\t- the rung range from the request's ladder line;\n    69\t- first-judgement and changed against the request's \"[Previous conclusion …]\" line;\n    70\t- non-empty stance fields;\n    71\t- duplicate theme pushes.\n    72\t\n    73\tThe archive's final ledger state is not used: it falsely refuses stored answers such as 8834 and 8851 (Codex r40).\n    74\t\n    75\t### Part B (48 answers)\n    76\tEach answer is read against `probe-targets.json`.\n    77\t- **Evidence targets.** One class each:\n    78\t  - *intended*: the fact is stated, a listed source in `sources` is cited or pushed, and no `near_misses` entry is cited for it;\n    79\t  - *unsupported*: stated, with no source;\n    80\t  - *misattributed*: a `near_misses` entry is cited or pushed while its quote carries no claim the answer states. This counts even if\n    81\t    a correct source is also cited;\n    82\t  - *omitted*: the fact is not stated.\n    83\t  A `markers` search in the answer's text flags the reader's attention; the hand reading decides.\n    84\t- **Placement targets.**\n    85\t  - *correct*: the id appears only in its own field;\n    86\t  - *absent*: not named;\n    87\t  - a substitute evidence id carrying no claim the answer states is *misattributed*.\n    88\t- **Ambiguous targets** (two acceptable sources, or an unclear fact) are reported and count neither way.\n    89\t- **A second, blind reader** (Codex) reads the 48 against the frozen targets. A disagreement goes to a third, source-backed read whose\n    90\t  class decides.\n    91\t\n    92\t### Part C (12 comparisons, every one read)\n    93\tThe numbers are only for prioritising. Each request's reading covers:\n    94\t- the sources cited by at least 2 of 3 answers in one arm and at most 1 of 3 in the other, each read beside its quote and the\n    95\t  answers' reasons;\n    96\t- any v12 rung outside the range of the v7 rungs.\n    97\t\n    98\tA **material harm** is any of these, repeated (in at least 2 of 3 v12 answers and at most 1 of 3 v7 answers):\n    99\t- (a) a source that at least 2 of 3 v7 answers cite for a fact their reasons state, which at least 2 of 3 v12 answers neither cite\n   100\t  nor replace with a listed source carrying that fact, where the fact moves the forecast or the reference;\n   101\t- (b) a source cited by at least 2 of 3 v12 answers whose quote carries no claim their reasons state;\n   102\t- (c) a v12 rung outside the v7 range whose reasons trace to a citation difference of kind (a) or (b).\n   103\t\n   104\tA difference that is not repeated, or whose cause cannot be read, is **unresolved**.\n   105\t\n   106\t### Outcome (registered; proposed tolerances for this probe, not historical rates)\n   107\t- **Pass (\"ready to propose adoption\"),** when every condition holds:\n   108\t  - Q1: A1 and A0 return 200 with format-valid answers;\n   109\t  - all 48 Part B answers and all 12 Part C comparisons (72 answers) are complete;\n   110\t  - no v12 answer is refused by the request-time check;\n   111\t  - in Part B, at most 2 of 48 answers have a confirmed misattribution (an answer counts once), with the number of requests affected\n   112\t    and any repetition reported;\n   113\t  - no confirmed material loss in Part B: a material target omitted in all 3 draws, which the stored accepted v7 answer to the same\n   114\t    request stated and supported;\n   115\t  - no confirmed material harm in Part C.\n   116\t- **Inconclusive:**\n   117\t  - a budget stop, a missing answer, a comparison not started;\n   118\t  - an unresolved material loss in Part B (a material target omitted in all 3 draws where no stored accepted v7 answer to that request\n   119\t    kept it, as in control-1's 8938, whose retry also dropped 85.4%);\n   120\t  - an unresolved Part C difference that would be material if confirmed.\n   121\t- **Not ready:** any other failure of the pass conditions, with its reason.\n   122\t- Every outcome goes to Alex; adoption is his decision.\n   123\t\n   124\t## 4. Harness and the $0 rehearsal before the send\n   125\t\n   126\t`probe.py`, harness-only:\n   127\t- it reads `probe-plan.json` and builds each request with `model.build_request`, from poc-v121's setting and v12's format (or v7's\n   128\t  for the control);\n   129\t- it sends through `budget_replay.BudgetTransport` on a ledger at $0.50, in the order and under the start rule of §2;\n   130\t- each answer goes through `model.call`'s format check and the request-time check;\n   131\t- each answer is written to `probe-answers.jsonl` with its part, request, format, draw, the request sha, the cost and the transport's\n   132\t  call number.\n   133\t- `probe_read.py` builds the reading packets: Part B by target, with marker flags; Part C by comparison, with the\n   134\t  symmetric-difference sources and their quotes.\n   135\t\n   136\t**The $0 rehearsal** uses the real budget transport with the provider stubbed underneath (`model.openai_transport` replaced), on a\n   137\tledger file of its own. It must show:\n   138\t- every scheduled send reaches the stub, with a count equal to the plan: 2 + 1 for A, 48 for B, 72 for C;\n   139\t- each request's bytes: system and user equal the stored ones, the format strict and equal to `schema_for('judging-v12', lists)` (or\n   140\t  v7's), the model settings as poc-v121's;\n   141\t- three fresh stub calls per draw set;\n   142\t- the request-time check equals `check_answer` on a ledger fixture whose state is known, accepts every stored committed answer of the\n   143\t  planned requests, and refuses the stored refused ones on their ids;\n   144\t- the stub's valid answer, a format-invalid answer, an HTTP 400 and a 5xx followed by success each take their path;\n   145\t- a ceiling set low stops a Part C comparison midway, and `probe_read.py` then reports \"inconclusive\", never \"pass\";\n   146\t- the Part B packet for control-1's 8938 flags a stub answer that drops \"85.4\" as a possible omission of a material target.\n   147\t\n   148\tA mutant, Part B sent with the open format, is caught by the byte check.\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff  1906932 Nov 22 00:36 docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe-plan.json\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff     7316 Nov 22 00:34 docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe-targets.json\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff     7284 Nov 22 00:36 docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe_plan.py\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'def (rungs_of|current_state)|create table.*evidence|create table.*judgements' src/hivemind/views/store.py; sed -n '253,289p' src/hivemind/model.py; sed -n '266,290p' clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/budget_replay.py; sed -n '259,305p' src/hivemind/views/judging.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'def (rungs_of|current_state)|create table.*evidence|create table.*judgements' src/hivemind/views/store.py; sed -n '253,289p' src/hivemind/model.py; sed -n '266,290p' clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/budget_replay.py; sed -n '259,305p' src/hivemind/views/judging.py\"","aggregated_output":"97:def rungs_of(conn, view_id):\n234:def current_state(conn, view_id):\ndef call(archive, cfg, setting, system, user, schema, schema_name, purpose, *, transport=None):\n    provider = cfg.provider\n    request = build_request(setting, system, user, schema, schema_name, provider)\n    request_json, sha = request_identity(request)\n    hit = archive.lookup(sha)\n    prompt_chars = len(system) + len(user)\n    if hit:\n        call_id, answer_json, _, input_tokens, output_tokens = hit\n        return Answer(json.loads(answer_json), call_id, sha, True, prompt_chars, input_tokens or 0, output_tokens or 0, 0.0)\n    started = time.time()\n    response = (transport or openai_transport)(provider, request)\n    seconds = round(time.time() - started, 1)\n    try:\n        content = response['choices'][0]['message']['content']\n        data = json.loads(content)\n    except (KeyError, IndexError, TypeError, json.JSONDecodeError) as error:\n        archive.record(purpose=purpose, config_version=cfg.version, config_checksum=cfg.checksum, model=setting.model,\n                       request_sha256=sha + ':malformed:' + uuid.uuid4().hex[:8], request_json=request_json,\n                       response_json=json.dumps(response)[:200000], answer_json=None, prompt_chars=prompt_chars,\n                       input_tokens=0, output_tokens=0, cost_usd=0.0, seconds=seconds, created_at=time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S'))\n        raise SchemaError(f'malformed answer: {error}')\n    errors = check_schema(data, schema)\n    usage = response.get('usage') or {}\n    input_tokens = int(usage.get('prompt_tokens') or 0)\n    output_tokens = int(usage.get('completion_tokens') or 0)\n    cached_tokens = min(input_tokens, int((usage.get('prompt_tokens_details') or {}).get('cached_tokens') or 0))\n    price_in, price_out = PRICES.get(setting.model, (0.0, 0.0))\n    cost = round((input_tokens - cached_tokens) / 1e6 * price_in + cached_tokens / 1e6 * price_in * CACHED_INPUT_SHARE + output_tokens / 1e6 * price_out, 6)\n    call_id = archive.record(purpose=purpose, config_version=cfg.version, config_checksum=cfg.checksum, model=setting.model,\n                             request_sha256=sha if not errors else sha + ':schema:' + uuid.uuid4().hex[:8], request_json=request_json,\n                             response_json=json.dumps(response)[:200000], answer_json=json.dumps(data, ensure_ascii=False),\n                             prompt_chars=prompt_chars, input_tokens=input_tokens, output_tokens=output_tokens, cost_usd=cost,\n                             seconds=seconds, created_at=time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S'))\n    if errors:\n        raise SchemaError('; '.join(errors[:5]))\n    return Answer(data, call_id, sha, False, prompt_chars, input_tokens, output_tokens, cost)\n        self.ledger, self.sleep = ledger, sleep\n\n    def __call__(self, *args):\n        if len(args) == 1:\n            return self._jev(args[0])\n        provider, request = args\n        for attempt in range(3):\n            estimate = llm_estimate(request)\n            if not self.ledger.reserve(estimate):\n                raise model.TransportError('the shared budget ceiling would be passed: not sent')\n            try:\n                response = model.openai_transport(provider, request, max_attempts=1)\n            except model.TransportError as error:\n                self.ledger.settle(estimate, estimate)\n                status = getattr(error, 'http_status', None)\n                if status in CLIENT_ERRORS or attempt == 2 or (status is not None and status != 429 and status < 500):\n                    raise\n                self.sleep(5 * (attempt + 1))\n                continue\n            except Exception:\n                self.ledger.settle(estimate, estimate)\n                raise\n            cost = llm_cost(response, request)\n            self.ledger.settle(estimate, estimate if cost is None else cost)\n            return response\ndef check_answer(conn, view_id, data, request_text=None, lists=None):\n    \"\"\"request_text: the user message that was sent, so theme and evidence ids are checked against what it carried (a theme may have\n    closed or opened since); without it, against what the ledger would show now. lists (judging-v12, D713): the ids the request\n    listed, from the rows that rendered it, the same lists its answer format closed; membership is checked against them.\"\"\"\n    errors = []\n    if lists is not None:\n        shown = set(lists['themes'])\n    else:\n        shown = themes_shown_in(request_text) if request_text is not None else {theme['view_id'] for theme in themes_in_force(conn, view_id)}\n    named = []\n    for entry in data.get('theme_pushes', []):\n        if entry['view_id'] in named:\n            errors.append(f'{entry[\"view_id\"]} is named twice in theme_pushes')\n        elif entry['view_id'] not in shown:\n            errors.append(f'{entry[\"view_id\"]} is not a theme in force shown to {view_id}')\n        named.append(entry['view_id'])\n    # the closed set is what the request showed (D198), read from the text that was sent, else computed from the ledger\n    if lists is not None:\n        allowed, deps = set(lists['evidence']), set(lists['dependencies'])\n    else:\n        allowed = evidence_shown_in(request_text) if request_text is not None else {p['evidence_id'] for p in evidence_in_view(conn, view_id)[0]}\n        deps = {d['resolved_to'] for d in store.dependencies_of(conn, view_id) if d['resolved_to']}\n    not_allowed = lambda e: f'{e} is not among the evidence shown to {view_id}'\n    bad_push = lambda e: f'push on {e} which was not shown to {view_id}'\n    rungs = store.rungs_of(conn, view_id)\n    if not 1 <= data['rung'] <= len(rungs):\n        errors.append(f'rung {data[\"rung\"]} outside 1..{len(rungs)}')\n    for cited in data['cites']:\n        if cited not in allowed:\n            errors.append(not_allowed(cited))\n    for cited in data['cites_views']:\n        if cited not in deps:\n            errors.append(f'{cited} is not a dependency of {view_id}')\n    for push in data['pushes']:\n        if push['evidence_id'] not in allowed:\n            errors.append(bad_push(push['evidence_id']))\n    for field in STANCE_FIELDS:\n        if not str(data.get(field) or '').strip():\n            errors.append(f'{field} is empty: state what the market expects and the figure or date the evidence implies')\n    last = store.current_state(conn, view_id)\n    if last is None and not data['changed']:\n        errors.append('first judgement must say changed')\n    if last is not None and last['rung'] != data['rung'] and not data['changed']:\n        errors.append(f'rung moved from {last[\"rung\"]} to {data[\"rung\"]} but changed is false')\n    return errors\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,re,json,sqlite3\nfrom pathlib import Path\nh=Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists'\\\\'').resolve()\nsys.path[:0]=[str(h),str(Path('\\\\''src'\\\\'').resolve())]\nimport t2_offline as t\np=json.loads((h/'\\\\''probe-plan.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nd={label:sqlite3.connect(f'\\\\''file:{folder/\\\"archive.sqlite\\\"}?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True) for label,folder,top in t.SOURCES}\nfor name,i,retry,terms in [\n ('\\\\''old-2'\\\\'',6955,6979,r'\\\\''definitive agreement|entered into a merger agreement|SEC filing|\"'^'\"\\\\- e3800 |\"'^'\"\\\\- e3965 '\\\\''),\n ('\\\\''control-1'\\\\'',8807,8809,r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$393 billion'\"'\\\\''),\n ('\\\\''old-3'\\\\'',6236,6244,r'\\\\''(?:\\\\\"'$350 billion|''^'\"\\\\- e138[0125] )'\\\\''),\n ('\\\\''old-3'\\\\'',6458,6459,r'\\\\''deployed by|shipments already underway'\\\\'')]:\n    raw, ans=d[name].execute('\\\\''select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(i,)).fetchone()\n    ar=json.loads(d[name].execute('\\\\''select answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(retry,)).fetchone()[0])\n    a=json.loads(ans)\n    ui=json.loads(raw)['\\\\''messages'\\\\''][1]['\\\\''content'\\\\'']\n    blocks=[x for x in re.split(r'\\\\''(?m)(?=\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[)'\\\\'',ui) if re.match(r'\\\\''\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\['\\\\'',x)]\n    print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',name,i,retry)\n    print('\\\\''retry prose'\\\\'',ar['\\\\''reasons'\\\\''])\n    for b in blocks:\n        b=b.split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\n['\\\\'',1)[0]; ls=b.splitlines(); k=ls[0].split()[1]\n        if re.search(terms,'\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\''.join(ls[:2]),re.I):\n            print(k,'\\\\''original/retry cite'\\\\'',k in a['\\\\''cites'\\\\''],k in ar['\\\\''cites'\\\\''],'\\\\''retry push'\\\\'',next((z['\\\\''push'\\\\''] for z in ar['\\\\''pushes'\\\\''] if z['\\\\''evidence_id'\\\\'']==k),'\\\\'''\\\\''),ls[1] if len(ls)>1 else ls[0])\nprint('\\\\''snapshot'\\\\'',p['\\\\''acceptance names'\\\\''],[(r['\\\\''source'\\\\''],r['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],r['\\\\''estimated cost'\\\\'']) for r in p['\\\\''acceptance'\\\\'']],len(p['\\\\''matched'\\\\'']),p['\\\\''estimate'\\\\''])\nm=next(r for r in p['\\\\''matched'\\\\''] if r['\\\\''source'\\\\'']=='\\\\''control-1'\\\\'' and r['\\\\''call_id'\\\\'']==8790); a=json.loads(m['\\\\''stored answer'\\\\'']); print('\\\\''C example'\\\\'',a['\\\\''reasons'\\\\''],a['\\\\''rung'\\\\''],a['\\\\''cites'\\\\''],a['\\\\''cites_views'\\\\''],a['\\\\''reference'\\\\''],a['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,re,json,sqlite3\nfrom pathlib import Path\nh=Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists'\\\\'').resolve()\nsys.path[:0]=[str(h),str(Path('\\\\''src'\\\\'').resolve())]\nimport t2_offline as t\np=json.loads((h/'\\\\''probe-plan.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nd={label:sqlite3.connect(f'\\\\''file:{folder/\\\"archive.sqlite\\\"}?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True) for label,folder,top in t.SOURCES}\nfor name,i,retry,terms in [\n ('\\\\''old-2'\\\\'',6955,6979,r'\\\\''definitive agreement|entered into a merger agreement|SEC filing|\"'^'\"\\\\- e3800 |\"'^'\"\\\\- e3965 '\\\\''),\n ('\\\\''control-1'\\\\'',8807,8809,r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$393 billion'\"'\\\\''),\n ('\\\\''old-3'\\\\'',6236,6244,r'\\\\''(?:\\\\\"'$350 billion|''^'\"\\\\- e138[0125] )'\\\\''),\n ('\\\\''old-3'\\\\'',6458,6459,r'\\\\''deployed by|shipments already underway'\\\\'')]:\n    raw, ans=d[name].execute('\\\\''select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(i,)).fetchone()\n    ar=json.loads(d[name].execute('\\\\''select answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(retry,)).fetchone()[0])\n    a=json.loads(ans)\n    ui=json.loads(raw)['\\\\''messages'\\\\''][1]['\\\\''content'\\\\'']\n    blocks=[x for x in re.split(r'\\\\''(?m)(?=\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[)'\\\\'',ui) if re.match(r'\\\\''\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\['\\\\'',x)]\n    print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',name,i,retry)\n    print('\\\\''retry prose'\\\\'',ar['\\\\''reasons'\\\\''])\n    for b in blocks:\n        b=b.split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\n['\\\\'',1)[0]; ls=b.splitlines(); k=ls[0].split()[1]\n        if re.search(terms,'\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\''.join(ls[:2]),re.I):\n            print(k,'\\\\''original/retry cite'\\\\'',k in a['\\\\''cites'\\\\''],k in ar['\\\\''cites'\\\\''],'\\\\''retry push'\\\\'',next((z['\\\\''push'\\\\''] for z in ar['\\\\''pushes'\\\\''] if z['\\\\''evidence_id'\\\\'']==k),'\\\\'''\\\\''),ls[1] if len(ls)>1 else ls[0])\nprint('\\\\''snapshot'\\\\'',p['\\\\''acceptance names'\\\\''],[(r['\\\\''source'\\\\''],r['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],r['\\\\''estimated cost'\\\\'']) for r in p['\\\\''acceptance'\\\\'']],len(p['\\\\''matched'\\\\'']),p['\\\\''estimate'\\\\''])\nm=next(r for r in p['\\\\''matched'\\\\''] if r['\\\\''source'\\\\'']=='\\\\''control-1'\\\\'' and r['\\\\''call_id'\\\\'']==8790); a=json.loads(m['\\\\''stored answer'\\\\'']); print('\\\\''C example'\\\\'',a['\\\\''reasons'\\\\''],a['\\\\''rung'\\\\''],a['\\\\''cites'\\\\''],a['\\\\''cites_views'\\\\''],a['\\\\''reference'\\\\''],a['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"\n old-2 6955 6979\nretry prose The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or signing deadline is supplied, so the status quo is no agreement by the due date. NVIDIA's filing says it entered into a definitive agreement on 2026-09-02, establishing the rung 1 outcome before 2027-02-26. The expected first-half-2027 closing date does not change this question, which asks whether an agreement is signed.\ne3768 original/retry cite True True retry push toward_first     Quote: On September 2, 2026, NVIDIA Corporation (“NVIDIA”) entered into a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face, Inc.\ne3800 original/retry cite False False retry push      Quote: Shares ⁠in Nvidia were ⁠slightly lower ​after the $12.93bn (£9.57bn) deal – which ranks among its biggest ever – was announced for the database of AI models on Thursday.\ne3908 original/retry cite False False retry push      Quote: The transaction is expected to close in the first half of 2027, according to an SEC filing .\ne3956 original/retry cite False False retry push      Quote: Nvidia confirmed Thursday that it has entered into a definitive agreement to acquire the open-source AI platform Hugging Face for $12.93 billion\ne3965 original/retry cite False False retry push      Quote: Huang's wealth is linked to his roughly 3% stake in Nvidia, which agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion .\n\n control-1 8807 8809\nretry prose The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3. Starting with reported Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carrying the 11% sequential growth implied by the Q2 guide through Q3 and Q4, gives a guidance-led baseline of about $386 billion. I forecast about $430 billion: confirmed Blackwell/Rubin orders and strong customer spending support an uplift over that baseline, but the orders span products and periods, while memory constraints and the exclusion of China compute sales temper the uplift. The estimate is above $400 billion but below $450 billion.\ne195 original/retry cite True True retry push none     Quote: The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\ne669 original/retry cite False True retry push none     Quote: According to Wall Street's average estimates (provided by Yahoo! Finance), Nvidia's revenue could grow to $393 billion during its current 2027 fiscal year, and then to $559 billion in fiscal 2028.\n\n old-3 6236 6244\nretry prose The reference is rung 2 (No): no consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated deadline for signing is supplied, so the status-quo expectation is no agreement by July 27, 2027. The specific reports of discussions to finance up to $350 billion of OpenAI chip purchases make signing some amount by the deadline more likely than not, and the stated scale of planned NVIDIA deployments supports underlying demand. The reports remain about discussions, not a signed agreement; the talks are described as early-stage and capable of collapsing. Because rung 1 requires any amount, not the reported $350 billion ceiling, the forecast still crosses to Yes.\ne1197 original/retry cite True True retry push toward_first     Quote: Alongside this, The Wall Street Journal reported that the chipmaker is also concurrently discussing financing OpenAI's chip purchases worth up to an additional $350 billion.\ne1291 original/retry cite False False retry push      Quote: July 26 : The WSJ also reported that Nvidia is discussing up to $350 billion in financing to help OpenAI purchase its AI chips\ne1295 original/retry cite True True retry push none     Quote: a separate $350 billion financing package to help OpenAI purchase Nvidia chips.\ne1337 original/retry cite False False retry push      Quote: The report also said Nvidia is in discussions to finance a $350 billion chip purchase for OpenAI.\ne1371 original/retry cite False False retry push      Quote: In a parallel negotiation, Nvidia is weighing a separate deal to finance up to $350 billion in GPU purchases by OpenAI, which would bring total Nvidia financial exposure to the project to roughly $600 billion [3] .\ne1379 original/retry cite True False retry push      Quote: Nvidia is also in discussions to finance $350 billion of OpenAI’s purchases of its chips for the US project, according to a person familiar with the matter.\ne1380 original/retry cite False False retry push      Quote: A prospective deal under discussion with OpenAI would help the ChatGPT creator lease a $500 billion, 10-gigawatt hub that SB Energy, a SoftBank Group Corp. subsidiary, is developing in Ohio, people familiar with the matter said.\ne1381 original/retry cite True True retry push toward_last     Quote: Negotiations are in their early stages and could collapse or financing terms may change, the people said.\ne1385 original/retry cite False False retry push      Quote: SoftBank has committed nearly $65 billion in investments to OpenAI alone by October and has signed a $40 billion bridge loan — one of the largest-ever bridge financings in the Asia-Pacific region — to finance that investment.\ne1404 original/retry cite False False retry push      Quote: At the same time, NVIDIA is reportedly in discussions to help fund an additional $350 billion in chips for OpenAI.\ne1422 original/retry cite False False retry push      Quote: The New York Times reports that Nvidia is separately evaluating financing approximately US$350 billion worth of AI chips for the Ohio facility.\ne1426 original/retry cite False False retry push      Quote: a separate arrangement that could help finance up to $350 billion of OpenAI chip purchases\ne1509 original/retry cite True False retry push      Quote: Nvidia is reportedly in talks to guarantee as much as $250 billion in data center lease payments so OpenAI can use a SoftBank facility, on top of up to $350 billion in GPU purchase financing for OpenAI.\ne1740 original/retry cite True False retry push      Quote: Nvidia was also in discussions to finance US$350 billion of OpenAI’s purchases of its chips for the project, people familiar with the situation said at the time.\ne1878 original/retry cite False False retry push      Quote: Nvidia was also in discussions to finance US$350bil of OpenAI’s purchases of its chips for the project, people familiar with the situation said at the time, asking not to be identified as the talks were private.\ne1984 original/retry cite True False retry push      Quote: This is in addition to the reported talks to backstop up to $250B of OpenAI’s compute leases at the SB Energy 10GW Ohio campus and to finance ~$350B of OpenAI chip purchases.\n\n old-3 6458 6459\nretry prose The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2; it is not a total-CPU figure. Adding Grace's reported trailing-12-month CPU revenue of more than $5 billion as the current run-rate implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion, crossing the rung 1 boundary. The 2026 shipment estimate is a counterweight, but the later company guidance and production/shipment updates carry more weight.\ne3083 original/retry cite True True retry push toward_first     Quote: Kress said Wednesday that Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by “every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, with shipments already underway to our lead partners,” which include Oracle and SpaceXAI.\nsnapshot ['A1', 'A0', 'A2'] [('old-1', 5652, 0.007428), ('old-3', 69, 0.000448), ('sealed-D358', 6918, 0.011941)] 12 0.3589\nC example The guidance-led run-rate is below $400 billion: Q1's $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the guided 11% sequential growth into Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion for FY2027. New GPU deployment, production and market-access signals tilt the forecast modestly above that baseline, while China restrictions and memory-supply constraints offset some of the upside; the discounted, multi-period targets do not justify a forecast above $400 billion. 3 ['e195', 'e458', 'e851', 'e234', 'e599', 'e924', 'e975', 'e949', 'e284', 'e252', 'e253', 'e376', 'e321', 'e231'] [] Bloomberg's FY2027 estimate of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The Q1 actual and Q2 guidance, with 11% sequential growth carried through the remaining quarters, imply about $386 billion. Allowing a modest uplift for disclosed deployment and production signals, but discounting targets spanning multiple products or periods and balancing supply and China risks, the central forecast is about $390 billion.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
